{"name":"Lady Banksia","hex":"#fde5a7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lady","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The mistress of a household.","A woman of breeding or higher class, a woman of authority."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To address as “lady”."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lady"},{"term":"banksia","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant belonging to the genus Banksia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/banksia"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lady","blocks":[{"text":"A lady is a woman who behaves in a polite way. Once used to describe only women of a high social class or status, the female counterpart of lord, now it may refer to any adult woman, as gentleman can be used for men."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady","wikidata":"Q1378024"},{"title":"Banksia","blocks":[{"text":"Banksia is a genus of around 170 species of flowering plants in the family Proteaceae. These Australian wildflowers and popular garden plants are easily recognised by their characteristic flower spikes, and woody fruiting \"cones\" and heads. Banksias range in size from prostrate woody shrubs to trees up to 30 metres (100 ft) tall. They are found in a wide variety of landscapes: sclerophyll forest, (occasionally) rainforest, shrubland, and some more arid landscapes, though not in Australia's deserts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Banksia_in_the_Blue_Mountains.jpg/330px-Banksia_in_the_Blue_Mountains.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banksia","wikidata":"Q131258"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg/500px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.